item.description:
arXiv:2610.09338v2 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: We construct fully deterministic finite-dimensional spaces for Sobolev approximation using shallow neural networks with a localized activation $\sigma$, either Gaussian or tanh-difference. For a bounded Lipschitz domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb R^d$, $d\ge2$, and a prescribed regularity index $s>0$, the approximation space at frequency resolution $M$ is\[
  V_M^\Omega=\operatorname{span}\left\{
  \sigma\!\left(\frac{\omega_i\cdot x-b_{j,M}}{\varepsilon_M}\right):
  1\le i\le J_M,\quad 1\le j\le K_{i,M}
  \right\}. \]All parameters are prescribed explicitly and independently of the target function. The offset cutoff depends on the direction and the domain, with an exterior margin tending to zero. The feature count satisfies $N_M=\sum_{i=1}^{J_M}K_{i,M}\asymp M^d$. For sufficiently large $M$, we construct a bounded linear operator $\mathcal A_M^\Omega:H^s(\Omega)\to V_M^\Omega$ that achieves the optimal approximation rate \[
  \|u-\mathcal A_M^\Omega u\|_{H^m(\Omega)}
  \le C N_M^{-(s-m)/d}\|u\|_{H^s(\Omega)} \] for every $u\in H^s(\Omega)$ and every integer $0\le m<s$. Numerical experiments in two and three dimensions illustrate the approximation behavior in $L^2$ and $H^1$.

arXiv:2610.09357v2 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: This paper gives bounds on the spectral condition numbers of Sobolev Gram matrices for deterministic ridge features with Gaussian or tanh-difference activations. In earlier work we showed that the shallow neural network spaces spanned by these features attain optimal Sobolev approximation rates. For either activation $\sigma$, the construction uses $N_{\sigma,M}\asymp M^d$ features at resolution $M$ on a bounded connected Lipschitz domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d$, $d\ge2$. For each fixed integer $m\ge0$ and all sufficiently large $M$, the raw $H^m(\Omega)$ Gram matrix $G$ and its column-normalized counterpart $\widetilde{G}$ are positive definite and satisfy \[
  \max\{\kappa_2(G),\kappa_2(\widetilde{G})\}
  \le \exp(CM)\quad (d=2), \] and \[
  \max\{\kappa_2(G),\kappa_2(\widetilde{G})\}
  \le \exp(CM\log(M+1))\quad (d\ge3), \] where $C$ is independent of $M$. On the unit ball in every dimension $d\ge3$, matching lower bounds show that the order of the exponent is sharp for both matrices.
